WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.666 --> 00:00:03.249
(gentle music)

00:00:09.212 --> 00:00:11.020
- I got rear-ended,

00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:14.867
and I couldn't use my right
hand for anything intricate.

00:00:16.124 --> 00:00:20.124
("Re:Member" by Ólafur Arnalds)

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:26.150
It wasn't really until I
started finding these ways

00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:29.950
to work around it that
I got my passion back

00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:31.643
and my positivity back for music.

00:00:32.620 --> 00:00:37.250
It's such a unique
thing to hear a software

00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:40.070
controlling something so physical.

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:42.770
People think that there might
be a ghost playing that.

00:00:46.930 --> 00:00:48.310
My name is Ólafur Arnalds.

00:00:48.310 --> 00:00:52.013
I am a producer, songwriter,
composer from Iceland.

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:55.970
I approach my music like
an electronic producer.

00:00:55.970 --> 00:00:59.050
I work in the computer
with a lot of synthesizers

00:00:59.050 --> 00:01:02.300
and drum machines, but I'm
using classical instruments

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:04.093
like strings and piano.

00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:10.930
I do like to bring all
these traditional sounds

00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:14.660
of strings, of woodwinds,
physical instruments

00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:17.950
that are so expressive, so emotive,

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:22.420
into an environment that is
a little bit more accessible

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:23.253
for most people.

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:38.950
The STRATUS is a software
that I created with my friend

00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:43.620
and this software creates
generative algorithms

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:47.603
that are meant to control
these two self-playing pianos.

00:01:48.757 --> 00:01:51.840
(gentle piano music)

00:01:55.156 --> 00:01:59.120
These self-playing pianos
have been around for ages.

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:03.140
I always thought there's a beautiful piece

00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:06.320
of technology there
that is only being used

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:10.060
to play back music, but
not necessarily being used

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:11.823
very much to create music.

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:17.690
I insert notes and the
software reads those notes,

00:02:17.690 --> 00:02:20.700
so it knows what chord I'm playing.

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:25.540
So each note of the chord
gets its own rhythm,

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:27.873
tempo, time signature, and everything.

00:02:29.330 --> 00:02:34.250
If you give each note just a
very like kind of random number

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:38.963
not in any sort of four
four type of signature,

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:42.890
that's when you get these things
that sounds totally random

00:02:42.890 --> 00:02:44.860
but actually they're not.

00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:46.863
They're just very complex mathematics.

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:51.760
There's a lot of accidental patterns

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:55.620
that happen along the way,
which I then listen to

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:58.020
and grab onto if I hear
something interesting

00:02:58.020 --> 00:02:59.500
and then I can build the song

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:01.393
from those accidental patterns.

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:08.181
This is my song "Unfold"
from my new album Remember.

00:03:08.181 --> 00:03:11.931
("Unfold" by Ólafur Arnalds)

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:19.340
This song started with me
just kind of improvising

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:22.780
and playing around with
this STRATUS software

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:26.230
and seeing what randomized
elements the software

00:03:26.230 --> 00:03:29.770
is throwing at me and grabbing
onto some of those ideas.

00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:33.520
("Unfold" by Ólafur Arnalds)

00:03:41.570 --> 00:03:46.150
I decided to put staccato
strings as kind of the main

00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:48.350
other element in the
first half of the song.

00:03:57.412 --> 00:04:01.329
And then the woodwinds
bring the tension to it.

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:10.700
I think what provides a very
interesting contrast here,

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:14.530
I asked these string players
to play as robotically

00:04:14.530 --> 00:04:18.680
as they could, but on the other
hand we have this element,

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:22.530
which is made by a
software and that element

00:04:22.530 --> 00:04:26.010
actually becomes more
human than what the humans

00:04:26.010 --> 00:04:27.830
are playing on the strings
and that provides the

00:04:27.830 --> 00:04:30.320
kind of emotional aspect of the song.

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:34.070
("Unfold" by Ólafur Arnalds)

00:04:35.311 --> 00:04:38.228
(heavy rock music)

00:04:43.542 --> 00:04:46.210
So when I was younger I was in
a band called Fighting Shit.

00:04:46.210 --> 00:04:48.780
We were like trash punk band.

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:49.890
I played drums.

00:04:49.890 --> 00:04:51.343
We went at it pretty hard.

00:04:52.377 --> 00:04:54.360
(yelling rock music)

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:56.960
We made a lot of records and
kind of made a little career

00:04:56.960 --> 00:04:57.793
out of that.

00:05:01.369 --> 00:05:03.906
("Ekki Hugsa" by Ólafur Arnalds)

00:05:03.906 --> 00:05:06.000
I become interested in classical music

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:08.280
mostly through my
grandparents who were very big

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:11.370
classical music listeners,
but it wasn't really until I

00:05:11.370 --> 00:05:14.333
discovered film scores
that I wanted to make it.

00:05:16.540 --> 00:05:20.250
Not really having the musical
education to back it up,

00:05:20.250 --> 00:05:23.310
my creations became
kind of very simplistic,

00:05:23.310 --> 00:05:26.810
loopy, versions of that, that
are actually more related

00:05:26.810 --> 00:05:28.860
to modern punk music.

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:32.470
I think the result is what
become neoclassical music

00:05:32.470 --> 00:05:34.850
is basically people trying
to make classical music

00:05:34.850 --> 00:05:36.623
but not really knowing how to.

00:05:37.950 --> 00:05:40.740
The most important thing
I took from punk music

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:42.980
was the DIY ethos of everything.

00:05:42.980 --> 00:05:47.210
Nothing was relying on
management or labels.

00:05:47.210 --> 00:05:49.940
We really believed in
doing things ourselves.

00:05:49.940 --> 00:05:52.860
So then when I started
doing classical music,

00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:54.190
I kept that ethos.

00:05:54.190 --> 00:05:56.370
You know, I was doing completely
different kind of music

00:05:56.370 --> 00:05:59.590
suddenly, but nothing in
my head actually changed.

00:05:59.590 --> 00:06:02.020
I kept booking the same
kind of venues I was touring

00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:04.400
with my punk bands, but just
bringing a string quartet

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:05.900
and a small electric keyboard.

00:06:09.990 --> 00:06:11.910
("Unfold" by Ólafur Arnalds)

00:06:11.910 --> 00:06:15.390
So once I had this idea
which is the piano,

00:06:15.390 --> 00:06:19.620
the staccato strings, and then
the woodwinds supporting it,

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:22.310
that's when I started adding
more complimentary things

00:06:22.310 --> 00:06:23.260
like the bass line,

00:06:27.190 --> 00:06:30.163
which is actually a
polyphonic Korg keyboard.

00:06:32.570 --> 00:06:35.170
There's a melody that
kind of alternates between

00:06:35.170 --> 00:06:38.460
a piano that I actually
play, not an automatic piano,

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:42.372
and then some legato
parts in the woodwinds.

00:06:42.372 --> 00:06:46.520
("Unfold" by Ólafur Arnalds)

00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:49.730
And that was kind of enough to
have that very simple melody

00:06:49.730 --> 00:06:52.180
to kind of give the song
a little bit of meaning.

00:06:57.712 --> 00:06:59.300
Because of how the rhythms of the pianos

00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:00.890
are kind of unpredictable,

00:07:00.890 --> 00:07:04.980
I filled a empty toilet paper
roll with a bunch of rice

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:09.370
and hit it with a drumstick
and that kind of helps

00:07:09.370 --> 00:07:12.003
the pianos define their
rhythm a little bit.

00:07:15.902 --> 00:07:16.820
Yeah, it's a little bit ironic

00:07:16.820 --> 00:07:19.490
that your complimenting very expensive,

00:07:19.490 --> 00:07:22.978
nice, real instruments with
a roll of toilet paper.

00:07:22.978 --> 00:07:25.228
(laughing)

00:07:29.527 --> 00:07:31.560
I got rear-ended.

00:07:31.560 --> 00:07:33.190
Something that happens
all the time, but it

00:07:33.190 --> 00:07:35.490
was by a big semi truck,
so it was a bit harsh.

00:07:38.490 --> 00:07:42.050
After that, I suffered some
nerve damage in my spine,

00:07:42.050 --> 00:07:45.430
and I couldn't use my right
hand for anything intricate

00:07:45.430 --> 00:07:48.800
for like a year, or a year and a half.

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:50.083
I couldn't play piano.

00:07:53.330 --> 00:07:54.970
It was heartbreaking to me.

00:07:54.970 --> 00:07:56.990
It was really difficult.

00:07:56.990 --> 00:07:59.833
I felt horribly depressed, to be honest.

00:08:00.710 --> 00:08:03.690
And that ended up really
setting this idea completely

00:08:03.690 --> 00:08:05.883
in stone to use these self-playing pianos.

00:08:09.500 --> 00:08:14.140
I could play chords, but
I couldn't play any scales

00:08:14.140 --> 00:08:15.780
or like individual notes,

00:08:15.780 --> 00:08:20.660
so that influenced how we
programmed STRATUS to react to

00:08:20.660 --> 00:08:23.130
me entering chords and turning them

00:08:23.130 --> 00:08:26.033
into textures and rhythms.

00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:33.450
It gave me a little bit of a purpose

00:08:33.450 --> 00:08:36.300
to be creating something without having

00:08:36.300 --> 00:08:37.943
to use my hands that much.

00:08:39.500 --> 00:08:41.963
That whole thing brought life back to me.

00:08:48.749 --> 00:08:52.260
("Unfold" by Ólafur Arnalds)

00:08:52.260 --> 00:08:55.830
Instead of building to a traditional peak,

00:08:55.830 --> 00:08:58.503
it actually builds up and
then suddenly drops down.

00:09:01.720 --> 00:09:05.980
I came up with this idea of
adding a traditional hip-hop

00:09:05.980 --> 00:09:08.503
808 bass drum into it,

00:09:12.490 --> 00:09:15.429
which you don't hear very
often in classical music.

00:09:15.429 --> 00:09:16.262
(laughing)

00:09:16.262 --> 00:09:20.440
So at the same time as this
big fat hip-hop kick drum

00:09:20.440 --> 00:09:25.125
comes in, the pianos become so soft.

00:09:25.125 --> 00:09:29.630
("Unfold" by Ólafur Arnalds)

00:09:29.630 --> 00:09:32.400
My friend the singer songwriter SOHN,

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:35.460
he came over and I showed
him the very early demo

00:09:35.460 --> 00:09:38.720
of this song and he just started singing

00:09:38.720 --> 00:09:43.720
and this like beautiful angelic
choir arrangement came out

00:09:43.740 --> 00:09:44.793
totally on the spot.

00:09:46.251 --> 00:09:50.001
("Unfold" by Ólafur Arnalds)

00:09:56.741 --> 00:09:58.800
For me, the creative process,

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:01.030
you can, you can look at it
like at the circle of life.

00:10:01.030 --> 00:10:03.080
It's not just one thing that finishes.

00:10:03.080 --> 00:10:05.040
There's always a continuation of it.

00:10:05.040 --> 00:10:07.460
So this song is very
much about that circle.

00:10:07.460 --> 00:10:09.703
Birth and the rebirth of creativity.

00:10:11.261 --> 00:10:15.011
("Unfold" by Ólafur Arnalds)

00:10:23.833 --> 00:10:26.800
STRATUS and this whole idea is just a tool

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:28.920
that gives me another sound
that I wouldn't be able

00:10:28.920 --> 00:10:30.260
to make otherwise.

00:10:30.260 --> 00:10:33.050
It's not supposed to
replace humans in any way

00:10:33.050 --> 00:10:34.630
'cause music is about saying something.

00:10:34.630 --> 00:10:36.192
It's about expressing something,

00:10:36.192 --> 00:10:38.803
and the computer isn't
trying to say anything.

00:10:41.650 --> 00:10:43.500
And music without meaning is nothing.

